{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210731,"nonce":"0BLHrVek7JEOs/VNYpl8xI8az7w=","title":"AOT SM transform: clone blocks reached multiple resume entries with disjoint load scopes","message":"Fixed in commit 5989bfc. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n`proc::closure_50` and `events::method_0_13` AOT-build with cranelift \"undefined value vN in terminator\". After a half-day of digging, ruled out the Init↔Resume co-cloning hypothesis I was originally chasing.\n\n**Why:** before sinking more time into co-cloning fixes, lock down what is and isn't the root cause so the next pass goes straight to the right rewrite.\n\n**How to apply:** when proc/events build-fails resurface, do not chase Init↔Resume divergence (bb_X.result=v85 vs bb_Y.result=v49). The shape is real but it's a downstream symptom of multi-path load-scope mismatch — fixing it just changes the verifier's complaint from \"v85\" to \"v95\".\n\n\nclosure_50 (proc.spec) is a tight loop with two yielding `_fn.call()` sites at bb6 and bb9 of the pre-SM MIR. The SM transform processes them one at a time. After both iterations, the post-transform CFG has:\n\n- Multiple `CrossFnCallResume` blocks. Each has its own `resume_loads` (slot → fresh load ValueId).\n- Cloned join blocks (e.g., a clone of the loop-`bb11` tail) whose body references load ValueIds belonging to ONE resume's scope.\n\nThe breakage: the same clone block ends up reachable from TWO resume entries whose load scopes are disjoint. E.g., bb20's terminator passes `(v95, v50, v90, v91, v92, v93)` to its successor — those vids are loaded at bb30 (slots 5–15). But bb20 is also a successor of bb18 (which is bb19's done_block), and bb19's loads are slots 5–15 → v73–v83. On the bb19→bb18→bb20 path, v95/v90–v93 are never bound. Cranelift's verifier flags them.\n\nThe Init↔Resume divergence (bb27.result=v85 / bb35.result=v49) is real and points at the co-cloning hole, but co-cloning Init↔Resume **does not fix it**. After landing the co-cloning fix the same closure_50 fails with a different vid — proof that the bug pattern is \"block X is shared between multiple loads scopes,\" not \"Init/Resume out of sync.\"\n\n\nIdentical results — the co-cloning fix is net-zero on user-visible build outcomes:\n\n| Package | AOT | Tiered | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 32 packages (assert, fp, web, http, template, …) | RUN-PASS | RUN-PASS | clean |\n| **hatch-io** | 42/44 | 44/44 | **AOT silent miscompile** (`Reader.withTryFn` multi-yield-in-loop) |\n| **proc, events** | BUILD-FAIL | RUN-PASS | AOT verifier reject |\n| audio, image, sqlite | \"fail\" | \"fail\" | not AOT — missing `libwlift_*.dylib` plugins, fails identically in all modes |\n\nio.spec is the third casualty of the same multi-path load-scope bug — same pattern (yield in a retry loop) lowered to AOT produces silently-wrong code instead of a verifier reject. Don't dismiss io's \"2 tests failing\" as functional flakes; they exercise the same SM-transform gap.\n\nThe Init↔Resume co-cloning patch was reverted (commit-not-landed) for that reason.\n\n\n`needs_clone_transitive` walks forward from `new_block_id` and clones blocks that use saved values (or transitively reach such blocks within the active region). But the joinblock case isn't covered:\n\n- A block bb_J that uses `v_load_X` (loaded at resume entry R_A) gets cloned correctly when iteration A processes — the clone references v_load_X.\n- After iteration A clones bb_J, the original CFG has bb_J reachable on path P_A (through R_A's done branch). But bb_J might ALSO be reachable on path P_B (through R_B's done branch from a different iteration). On P_B, v_load_X isn't bound.\n\nThe fix isn't more cloning — it's making sure every reachable path has the right loads. Two structural options:\n\n1. **Per-block load resolution**: rather than each Resume binding `val_map[vid] = ret`, give each block a \"reaching-load\" prologue that loads from slots based on which path got us here. Costs every clone block its own load preamble. Unsound under loop back-edges.\n\n2. **Process all yields first, then global clone+remap**: collect every yield's saves/loads as a planning pass; build a final CFG where every block-of-interest exists in N copies (one per reachable resume entry); apply remaps composed across all yields. This is the architectural rewrite the parent memo (project_aot_yield_architecture.md) describes as \"process yields FIRST, then clone+remap as a single global pass.\"\n\nOption 2 is the right one. Both `proc::closure_50` and `events::method_0_13` have multi-yield + loop + cross-fn shapes that incremental processing can't get right.\n\n\n`WLIFT_AOT_DUMP_FN=closure_50` dumps the SM-transformed MIR + layout. Useful fields:\n- `block_kinds[bb_X].result` — the ValueId cranelift binds at the matching Resume.\n- `resume_loads[bb_X]` — slot→ValueId table loaded at bb_X's prologue (Resume blocks).\n- Compare across Resumes to see when their load scopes diverge.\n\nFor the pre-SM MIR (to understand \"what was the original program\"), I temporarily added an early dump at `src/codegen/aot.rs:1234` (cloned MIR before `transform_to_state_machine`); landed and removed in the same session. Re-add when needed.\n\n\nWhen judging an AOT-only change, always run `--mode tiered` on the same spec to separate AOT bugs from cross-mode functional bugs. Native-plugin-missing failures (audio/image/sqlite) look identical to AOT bugs in audit output but reproduce in every mode. Misclassifying io.spec's two `Reader.withTryFn` failures as \"functional, not AOT\" was a real mistake — they're AOT silent miscompiles.","files":null}]}